{
  "requirement": "When authoring evidence for a named example, its complete fenced declaration\nMUST be included in the mandatory input set defined by\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the implementation and tests together satisfy `requirements.examples.authoring_inputs`. One test assertion is weaker than ideal, noted below.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `AssessmentSources::prepare` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` (lines 50–205) carries the `{impl}` reference. It accepts any non-term definition, including a named example, as the subject.\n- It always starts the selector list with `InputSelector::Requirements { requirements: subject_name }`. That selector is turned into a mandatory input by `source_input`, and it is pushed first, ahead of cited declarations, reference sources, anchors and term dependencies.\n- `InputSelector::resolve` (`src/inputs.rs`) builds the input text from `Declaration.raw_lines`, which covers all non-term kinds.\n- `collect_definitions` (`src/evidence.rs`) builds `raw_lines` from the whole block, opening fence through closing fence. It drops only `:impl:`/`:test:` option lines, which `requirements.references.anchors` treats as not part of the text. Anchors are contributed separately as file or node inputs.\n- `requirements.ci.requirements_inputs` defines the same \"complete raw fenced declaration, including opening and closing lines\" form, so this matches the definition of a complete fenced declaration.\n- The input set is assembled and supplied under `requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`. Generated evidence under `evidence/` is omitted only for outline selectors. A requirements selector is never dropped, so the example's declaration cannot be omitted. Existing evidence inputs are not carried forward.\n- `prepare` adds nothing the requirement does not specify. The extra behaviour (cited declarations, impl/test sources, terms) is attributed to other requirements through its `{impl}` markers.\n\n**Tests**\n- `bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors` (`src/authoring/inputs.rs`, lines 599–690) carries the `{test}` reference.\n  - It prepares assessment sources for the example `requirements.case`.\n  - It checks `subject_kind == Definition(Example)`.\n  - It checks that `mandatory_inputs()[0][\"requirements\"] == \"requirements.case\"`, so the example's declaration is the first mandatory input.\n- The text of that input is not asserted in this test. It comes from the shared `resolve` path.\n  - `requirements_inputs_omit_option_lines` (`src/evidence.rs`) checks `resolved.text == raw_lines`, including the fences.\n  - `tests/commands.rs` checks that a requirements input over `rules.*` stores `:::{example} case … :::` verbatim.\n  - `tests/authoring_cli.rs::initial_prompt_substitutes_subject_and_mandatory_inputs_into_the_template` runs an example subject (`requirements.ready_case`) through `habeas author` and asserts its `requirements` input is in the prompt. It carries no `{test}` reference to this requirement, so I used it only as supporting context.\n- The example-specific assertion is thin, but the behaviour is a single shared code path and the supporting tests cover the content, so I count the coverage as adequate.\n\n**Gaps:** none acknowledged with FIXME, and I found no unmarked implementation code.",
  "references": "b899630e41f440f0f3cdb55b167fbf32959bdbf515b06e0008b680021fa1d4d4",
